      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(web-fetch): recover from stale runtime providers",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nRuntime web-fetch metadata can retain a selected provider id that is no longer available in the current provider list. When the user did not explicitly configure that provider, resolution returned no provider instead of falling back to the current auto-detected provider.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe resolver now treats an explicit `providerId` as authoritative and fail-closed, while allowing stale runtime `selectedProvider` metadata to fall back to auto-detection. This keeps explicit config strict without letting old runtime state disable web fetch.\n\nWhen stale runtime metadata is normalized for the fallback provider, the provider-facing `selectedProviderKeySource` is also cleared. That source describes the selected provider's credential, so preserving `\"missing\"` or another source from the removed provider would mislead plugin runtime behavior.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers recover web fetch automatically after provider metadata becomes stale, while invalid explicit provider configuration still fails clearly. Providers no longer receive a fallback `selectedProvider` paired with a credential-source value from the removed provider.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=redacted-firecrawl-key node --import tsx - <<'NODE' ... NODE` against head `fe452da3b7502ec2e492b0c7924dc638851db06e`: real web-fetch runtime/provider discovery selected `firecrawl` from stale `removed-provider` runtime metadata, with the env key redacted.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs packages/web-content-core/src/provider-runtime-shared.test.ts src/web-fetch/runtime.test.ts -- --reporter=verbose` - 2 files / 32 tests passed.\n- `node_modules/.bin/oxfmt --check packages/web-content-core/src/provider-runtime-shared.ts packages/web-content-core/src/provider-runtime-shared.test.ts src/web-fetch/runtime.test.ts`\n- `OPENCLAW_OXLINT_SKIP_PREPARE=1 node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs packages/web-content-core/src/provider-runtime-shared.ts packages/web-content-core/src/provider-runtime-shared.test.ts src/web-fetch/runtime.test.ts`\n- `git diff --check`\n- `.agents/skills/autoreview/scripts/autoreview --mode local --engine claude` - clean, no accepted/actionable findings. Default Codex autoreview could not reach the model because the local OpenAI API key returned 401 before review.\n\n## Proof\n\nThe runtime proof used real `resolveWebFetchDefinition` and bundled Firecrawl provider discovery, not mocked provider lists. Output:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"env\": {\n    \"FIRECRAWL_API_KEY\": \"<redacted>\"\n  },\n  \"staleRuntimeSelectedProvider\": \"removed-provider\",\n  \"resolvedProvider\": \"firecrawl\",\n  \"toolDescription\": \"Fetch a page using Firecrawl.\",\n  \"originalRuntimeMetadataUnchanged\": {\n    \"providerConfigured\": \"removed-provider\",\n    \"providerSource\": \"configured\",\n    \"selectedProvider\": \"removed-provider\",\n    \"selectedProviderKeySource\": \"missing\",\n    \"diagnostics\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe focused regression tests cover both layers: the shared resolver clears stale `selectedProviderKeySource` in the normalized provider-facing metadata copy, and the web-fetch runtime still leaves the original runtime metadata object unchanged.\n",
        "branch": "llagy007/web-content-runtime-fallback-v2",
        "head_sha": "fe452da3b7502ec2e492b0c7924dc638851db06e",
